.TH SET_HAS_S 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_has_s \- Check if a string exists in a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/str.h>

.IB int
.IB set_has_s(const set_t* set, const char* s);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_has_s\fR function checks if a string exists in a set. It takes two arguments: the set and the string. The function returns 1 if the string exists in the set, or 0 otherwise.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set in which to check for the string.
.TP
.IB s
The string to check.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the string exists in the set, or 0 otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_has_s\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/str.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    const char* s = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    set_add(&set, s, str_len(s));

    if (set_has_s(&set, s)) {
        printf("String exists in set\n");
    } else {
        printf("String does not exist in set\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef